{"maxId":304,"entries":{"chapter-86-study-hepatic-pancreatic-and-biliary-diseases|Chapter 86 Study: Hepatic, Pancreatic, and Biliary Diseases":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Liver tests and coagulation factors generally do not change with age; functional hepatocyte volume and hepatic blood flow decline (p1337–1338).":["u3"],"copy|Phase I metabolism of some drugs falls by as much as 50%; phase II is relatively preserved. Start low and titrate slowly (p1338).":["u4"],"copy|Drug-induced liver injury is more frequent and severe in older adults; review prescription and nonprescription exposure (p1342).":["u5"],"copy|Metabolic syndrome raises fatty-liver risk. Alcohol abstinence can improve alcohol-associated disease even after decompensation (p1339–1340).":["u6"],"copy|PBC: cholestatic tests, antimitochondrial antibody; ursodeoxycholic acid slows progression. Autoimmune hepatitis requires biopsy; immunosuppression is often effective (p1342).":["u7"],"copy|PSC: evaluate with cholangiography, usually MRCP; manage cholangitis/strictures and monitor for cholangiocarcinoma (p1342–1343).":["u8"],"copy|Ascites, encephalopathy and frailty complicate cirrhosis care. Search for triggers of each encephalopathy episode; balance symptom benefit against treatment burden (p1343).":["u9"],"copy|Transplantation can suit selected older adults; age alone is not a contraindication (p1343).":["u10"],"copy|The key points say that older patients may lack fever, nausea, vomiting or severe abdominal pain (p1338). The diagnostic discussion also describes typical right-upper-quadrant or epigastric pain (p1345). Ultrasound is the initial investigation; ERCP is therapeutic for common-duct stones (p1345).":["u11"],"copy|Incidental asymptomatic gallstones do not require treatment. Early laparoscopic surgery suits appropriate patients; drainage may suit very ill patients (p1346).":["u12"],"copy|Table 86-7 describes fat, fibrosis and reduced pancreatic weight after the seventh decade, and steadily declining bicarbonate after the fourth decade (p1348). Pancreatic duct caliber can increase with age; frank exocrine deficiency is rare without underlying pathology.":["u13"],"copy|Acute pancreatitis: pain with amylase/lipase typically at least 3× normal; lipase remains elevated longer (p1348).":["u14"],"copy|Without gallstones or substantial alcohol exposure, consider underlying malignancy; check triglycerides early (p1348).":["u15"],"copy|Chronic pancreatitis: pain, malabsorption and diabetes; enzyme replacement treats malabsorption, and normal enzymes do not exclude disease (p1349).":["u16"],"copy|Pancreatic cancer needs staging for vascular invasion/metastases. Mucinous cysts/main-duct IPMN may warrant surgery in patients fit enough; branch-duct lesions may undergo surveillance (p1349–1350).":["u17"],"a-painful-gallbladder-in-a-frail-patient|A painful gallbladder in a frail patient":["u295"],"pancreatic-reserve-and-acute-pain|Pancreatic reserve and acute pain":["u296"],"interpreting-liver-tests-and-pharmacology-in-an-older-patient|Interpreting liver tests and pharmacology in an older patient":["u297"],"fat-accumulation-alcohol-and-medication-related-injury|Fat accumulation, alcohol and medication-related injury":["u298"],"viral-and-immune-mediated-disease|Viral and immune-mediated disease":["u299"],"managing-complications-and-recognizing-malignancy|Managing complications and recognizing malignancy":["u300"],"stones-duct-imaging-and-source-control|Stones, duct imaging and source control":["u301"],"from-loss-of-pancreatic-reserve-to-clinically-important-disease|From loss of pancreatic reserve to clinically important disease":["u302"],"chronic-pancreatitis-pancreatic-cancer-and-cysts|Chronic pancreatitis, pancreatic cancer and cysts":["u303"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u304"],"copy|The finding of a widened bile duct can reflect age or previous cholecystectomy; compare with prior imaging (p1344).":["u28"],"copy|Uncomplicated incidental gallstones and acute cholecystitis in a high-risk patient require different decisions (p1344–1346).":["u29"],"copy|A physiologic decline in exocrine reserve does not usually produce overt pancreatic insufficiency in a healthy older adult (p1348).":["u30"],"past-exam-questions|Past exam questions":["u31"],"recall-drill|Recall drill":["u32"],"copy|CHAPTER 86 / HAZZARD":["u21"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 86; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u22"],"copy|Liver aging & injury":["u23"],"copy|Chronic disease & malignancy":["u24"],"copy|Biliary disease":["u25"],"copy|Pancreas":["u26"],"copy|Incidental stones are common; a substantial new illness with right-upper-quadrant tenderness warrants a different assessment.":["u39"],"copy|Prolonged cystic-duct obstruction causes acute cholecystitis.":["u40"],"copy|Ultrasound can show stones, pericholecystic fluid, edema or a sonographic Murphy sign.":["u41"],"copy|When the gallbladder fails to fill on a HIDA scan despite opacification of the bile ducts, cystic-duct obstruction is supported.":["u42"],"copy|Fever, nausea or vomiting may be absent even with serious inflammation (p1344–1345).":["u43"],"copy|A common-duct stone may cause jaundice, dark urine, pale stools, pancreatitis or ascending cholangitis.":["u44"],"copy|Pain, fever and jaundice together suggest cholangitis.":["u45"],"copy|Ultrasound is the usual first study, but its reported sensitivity for common-duct stones is only 20% to 38%; duct dilatation alone is also nonspecific in older people.":["u46"],"copy|MRCP can clarify the anatomy, while ERCP can remove a stone or decompress infected bile (p1344–1345).":["u47"],"copy|For a frail, acutely unstable patient with acute cholecystitis, weigh the need for source control against operative risk.":["u48"],"copy|Fluids and antibiotics are part of treatment.":["u49"],"copy|Gallbladder drainage can provide source control when immediate surgery is unsafe.":["u50"],"copy|The book discusses early laparoscopic cholecystectomy for suitable operative candidates (p1346).":["u51"],"copy|Fat and fibrosis increase with age while pancreatic weight can fall.":["u52"],"copy|Bicarbonate output declines, yet frank malabsorption from normal aging alone is unusual.":["u53"],"copy|In an older patient with acute pancreatitis, gallstones remain an important cause.":["u54"],"copy|In the absence of gallstones or substantial chronic alcohol exposure, investigate other causes including occult malignancy.":["u55"],"copy|In chronic pancreatitis, serum enzymes may be normal even in someone with malabsorption (p1348–1349).":["u56"],"copy|Liver size and the volume of functioning hepatocytes decrease with age, and hepatic blood flow can be lower.":["u57"],"copy|Reserve at rest is often sufficient to maintain normal conventional liver chemistries and clotting-factor production.":["u58"],"copy|Table 86-1 describes a modest fall in albumin that generally remains within the normal range.":["u59"],"copy|A markedly low albumin, jaundice, prolonged coagulation tests or substantially raised liver enzymes therefore deserves a clinical explanation.":["u60"],"copy|Consider nutrition and inflammation alongside hepatic synthetic function.":["u61"],"copy|The same resting measurements cannot tell whether a patient will regenerate liver after resection or tolerate a serious toxic insult (p1337–1339).":["u62"],"copy|Phase I metabolism includes oxidation and other reactions that depend on the drug, enzyme system and hepatic extraction.":["u63"],"copy|Table 86-2 gives diazepam, alprazolam, selected tricyclic antidepressants, several statins, phenytoin, theophylline and warfarin as examples of medicines with substantial phase I handling.":["u64"],"copy|Relatively preserved phase II conjugation does not make every drug in a class interchangeable, and an age effect does not justify applying a fixed percentage reduction to every prescription.":["u65"],"copy|Assess the actual drug, other interacting medicines, signs of accumulation and the clinical response.":["u66"],"copy|Reduced hepatic blood flow can matter even if bilirubin and aminotransferases are normal (p1338–1339).":["u67"],"copy|Hazzard uses the terms NAFLD and NASH.":["u68"],"copy|In the chapter's definitions, more than 5% hepatic steatosis without the inflammatory injury of steatohepatitis differs from steatosis accompanied by inflammation and hepatocyte damage.":["u69"],"copy|The second pattern can progress to fibrosis, portal hypertension and liver failure.":["u70"],"copy|Many affected patients first present with metabolic risk factors, incidental fatty liver on imaging or a modest biochemical abnormality.":["u71"],"copy|Exclude substantial alcohol exposure, medication toxicity, viral disease and other causes before assigning etiology.":["u72"],"copy|Noninvasive fibrosis scores followed by elastography can help select patients needing further assessment; the amount of visible fat alone does not stage fibrosis (p1339).":["u73"],"copy|In one US multicenter comparison, older patients with fatty liver more often had steatohepatitis and advanced fibrosis than younger participants.":["u74"],"copy|This is a cohort finding, not a prediction of the stage in any one older adult.":["u75"],"copy|The book describes improved metabolic indices and liver chemistries with weight loss of more than 10% in patients with elevated BMI and metabolic syndrome.":["u76"],"copy|Ask how this goal interacts with muscle mass and nutritional status in a frail patient rather than interpreting better enzymes as the sole treatment objective.":["u77"],"copy|Continue to address the underlying metabolic illness and assess progression instead of assuming that identifying fatty liver completes the evaluation (p1339–1340).":["u78"],"copy|Alcohol-associated injury can be overlooked when history focuses exclusively on youth or occupational disability.":["u79"],"copy|Clarify current intake, the pattern over time and the patient's understanding of standard servings.":["u80"],"copy|In advanced disease, cessation can improve function even after prior decompensation.":["u81"],"copy|Treating ascites and encephalopathy without addressing continuing alcohol exposure leaves a driver of injury in place.":["u82"],"copy|Conversely, an abnormal enzyme result in a person reporting alcohol use is not proof of alcohol as the only cause.":["u83"],"copy|Consider metabolic, viral and drug-related contributors, and assess what support makes abstinence realistic (p1340, p1343).":["u84"],"copy|Table 86-3 organizes drug injury by pattern.":["u85"],"copy|Acetaminophen can produce dose-related hepatocellular necrosis, amoxicillin–clavulanate a cholestatic pattern, and isoniazid hepatocellular injury.":["u86"],"copy|Methotrexate is linked to steatosis and fibrosis; alcohol can compound the risk.":["u87"],"copy|Amiodarone can mimic fatty liver histologically and disease may progress despite discontinuation.":["u88"],"copy|Nitrofurantoin can cause a chronic hepatitis syndrome, an important connection in an older woman treated repeatedly for urinary symptoms.":["u89"],"copy|Tamoxifen, phenytoin and several antifungals can also cause injury.":["u90"],"copy|Establish start and stop dates, dosing history and nonprescription products.":["u91"],"copy|A list of potential culprits alone cannot establish causation when biliary obstruction, infection or another liver disease fits the presentation (p1339–1342).":["u92"],"copy|Table 86-4 distinguishes prior exposure, immunity and active disease.":["u93"],"copy|Acute hepatitis A is supported by an IgM response; IgG can follow earlier infection or vaccination.":["u94"],"copy|Hepatitis A does not cause chronic hepatitis in the chapter's comparison.":["u95"],"copy|For hepatitis B, surface antigen supports current infection and surface antibody can reflect immunity.":["u96"],"copy|Core antibody distinguishes natural exposure from vaccine-only immunity; ambiguous combinations need interpretation in context.":["u97"],"copy|In chronic hepatitis B, viral burden and the state of the liver help guide specialist evaluation.":["u98"],"copy|Hepatitis D is associated with hepatitis B; hepatitis E can merit consideration when other causes are excluded.":["u99"],"copy|The treatment and prevention options in the table were current when the chapter was written (p1340–1342).":["u100"],"copy|Hepatitis C antibody testing addresses exposure.":["u101"],"copy|Table 86-4 abbreviates a positive antibody result as infection and also lists quantitative RNA.":["u102"],"copy|For the individual patient, a positive antibody after successful therapy can persist, so establish present viremia before planning treatment.":["u103"],"copy|Direct-acting antivirals changed the prospects for cure in older people with chronic HCV; drug interactions and the stage of liver disease still affect selection.":["u104"],"copy|Liver injury due to another cause can persist after successful antiviral therapy.":["u105"],"copy|In particular, established cirrhosis remains clinically relevant when considering complications and surveillance (p1341–1342).":["u106"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): A reactive HCV antibody is followed by HCV RNA testing.":["u107"],"copy|Detectable RNA establishes current infection; reactive antibody with undetectable RNA usually indicates no current infection. - CDC hepatitis C testing guidance, 2025.":["u108"],"copy|Distinguish the predominantly small-duct autoimmune process of primary biliary cholangitis from large or medium duct scarring in primary sclerosing cholangitis.":["u109"],"copy|In PBC, a cholestatic pattern and the antimitochondrial M2 antibody can establish the diagnosis without routine biopsy in a typical case.":["u110"],"copy|Pruritus, fat-soluble vitamin malabsorption and osteoporosis need attention.":["u111"],"copy|Ursodeoxycholic acid slows disease progression.":["u112"],"copy|The book also names obeticholic acid as a possible add-on for selected patients, warning about greater itching and contraindication in decompensated disease.":["u113"],"copy|That historical option has changed in the United States (p1342).":["u114"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): The US approval for obeticholic acid for primary biliary cholangitis was withdrawn in November 2025. - FDA withdrawn indications, 2025.":["u115"],"copy|PSC instead calls for imaging of the biliary tree.":["u116"],"copy|MRCP can demonstrate the pattern without the procedural risk of ERCP; a dominant stricture or ascending cholangitis may require endoscopic intervention.":["u117"],"copy|Monitor for cholangiocarcinoma as well as the effects of progressive cholestasis.":["u118"],"copy|Autoimmune hepatitis has a different diagnostic route, with biopsy important to confirmation and assessment of active injury.":["u119"],"copy|Immunosuppression can be effective in older adults, but systemic glucocorticoid toxicity can include osteoporosis, impaired glucose tolerance and cataracts.":["u120"],"copy|Similar complaints of fatigue or itch do not make these three diagnoses interchangeable (p1342–1343).":["u121"],"copy|Figure 86-1 contrasts the tissue patterns in drug-induced liver injury.":["u122"],"copy|Methotrexate illustrates macrovesicular steatosis; valproate, tamoxifen and tetracyclines are associated with microvesicular steatosis and mitochondrial injury.":["u123"],"copy|Acetaminophen illustrates zone 3 hepatocellular necrosis, and the final panel shows necrosis from isoniazid.":["u124"],"copy|Use the exposure history alongside the biochemical and histologic pattern rather than equating fatty liver with a single cause (p1340).":["u125"],"copy|Recurrent ascites or hepatic hydrothorax can cause pain, dyspnea, reduced mobility and repeated admissions.":["u126"],"copy|Before escalating a diuretic, ask whether the patient can adhere to sodium restriction and reach a toilet, whether renal function or electrolytes are worsening and whether other disease is still injuring the liver.":["u127"],"copy|Large-volume paracentesis can relieve symptoms but entails repeat procedures and travel.":["u128"],"copy|A transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt can worsen encephalopathy.":["u129"],"copy|These tradeoffs matter when the patient prioritizes staying at home.":["u130"],"copy|Transplant evaluation considers reserve, comorbidity, functional status and ability to manage postoperative care alongside chronological age (p1343–1344).":["u131"],"copy|Do not attribute every new change in attention to pre-existing cognitive impairment or to irreversible terminal encephalopathy.":["u132"],"copy|A recurrent episode warrants a search for gastrointestinal hemorrhage, infection, electrolyte disturbance or sedating medicines.":["u133"],"copy|Intracranial bleeding may need to be considered in the appropriate presentation.":["u134"],"copy|Lactulose treatment can itself become difficult because of taste, flatulence, loose stool or fecal incontinence.":["u135"],"copy|Ask what the patient actually takes and what happens when doses are missed.":["u136"],"copy|Treatment planning can require help with medications, mobility, continence and decisions about driving rather than titration of a drug in isolation (p1343).":["u137"],"copy|Hepatocellular carcinoma often develops on a background of cirrhosis.":["u138"],"copy|The treatment decision depends on tumor burden, extrahepatic spread, vascular involvement and hepatic reserve.":["u139"],"copy|A localized lesion in a patient with adequate reserve presents a different question from extensive cancer in a person already decompensating.":["u140"],"copy|Cholangiocarcinoma can present through a dominant stricture, recurrent cholangitis or jaundice, particularly in PSC.":["u141"],"copy|Obtaining diagnostic tissue may be difficult because of access and the nature of the lesion.":["u142"],"copy|Gallbladder carcinoma may first be found at surgery or present with advanced obstructive disease.":["u143"],"copy|In each, ascertain both the potentially curative options and the likely burden of diagnostic or palliative intervention (p1343–1344).":["u144"],"copy|For HCC surveillance, the authors describe ultrasound and alpha-fetoprotein every 6 months for patients with cirrhosis while detection would still have clinical value.":["u145"],"copy|Surveillance and diagnostic staging are separate.":["u146"],"copy|A newly suspicious lesion calls for characterization; a serial AFP value or the fact that previous screening was normal does not establish resectability.":["u147"],"copy|In carefully selected older patients, a multidisciplinary team can consider resection, transplantation or local treatments such as radiofrequency ablation, chemoembolization or radioembolization.":["u148"],"copy|Consider tumor control alongside the probability of precipitating liver failure in the remaining liver (p1344).":["u149"],"copy|A dominant biliary stricture in PSC raises two immediate possibilities with overlapping symptoms: impaired drainage with cholangitis, and cholangiocarcinoma.":["u150"],"copy|Drainage may be urgent when infection is present while assessment for a tumor proceeds.":["u151"],"copy|Tissue diagnosis can be difficult because an infiltrating tumor may be sparsely cellular and inaccessible.":["u152"],"copy|For gallbladder malignancy, long-standing stones can be a risk association, but the high prevalence of benign stones makes a stone an unreliable cancer marker by itself.":["u153"],"copy|Interpret a mass, progressive obstruction or concerning wall changes in the complete clinical and imaging context (p1344).":["u154"],"copy|A stone in the gallbladder, a stone obstructing the cystic duct and a stone passing through the common duct create different syndromes.":["u155"],"copy|Incidentally detected gallbladder stones do not reliably explain diffuse nonspecific abdominal symptoms.":["u156"],"copy|Persistent cystic-duct obstruction can produce localized inflammation with continuous right-upper-quadrant or epigastric pain, sometimes radiating to the back or shoulder.":["u157"],"copy|Common-duct obstruction can present with jaundice, dark urine, pale stools and pruritus; adding fever and abdominal pain raises concern for ascending infection.":["u158"],"copy|Acalculous cholecystitis is particularly relevant in a severely ill or debilitated patient, where the absence of a stone can delay recognition (p1344–1345).":["u159"],"copy|Table 86-5 lists increasing age, female sex, obesity, dyslipidemia, rapid weight loss, certain medications and population associations as factors in gallstone formation.":["u160"],"copy|They alter the likelihood of stones, not the distinction between an incidental gallstone, cystic-duct inflammation and infection above an obstructed common duct (p1344).":["u161"],"copy|For suspected acute cholecystitis, ultrasound can show gallstones, edema, fluid around the gallbladder and focal tenderness under the probe.":["u162"],"copy|A HIDA study answers a different question: if the biliary tree fills but the gallbladder does not, cystic-duct obstruction is supported.":["u163"],"copy|Chronic cholecystitis may show less dramatic imaging changes.":["u164"],"copy|The printed chapter notes that older patients commonly still have focal pain even when fever, nausea and vomiting are absent.":["u165"],"copy|Do not turn the warning about atypical presentation into a rule that older adults do not experience localized pain (p1338, p1345).":["u166"],"copy|For suspected common-duct stones, ultrasound is convenient but its sensitivity is limited.":["u167"],"copy|A duct can be dilated because of age or previous cholecystectomy, so compare old imaging.":["u168"],"copy|CT can reveal complications or another diagnosis, and MRCP delineates the ducts noninvasively.":["u169"],"copy|In the chapter's cited estimates endoscopic ultrasound has 94% sensitivity and 95% specificity for choledocholithiasis.":["u170"],"copy|ERCP permits sphincterotomy, actual stone extraction and drainage of infected bile; it has procedure-related risks and need not be the initial purely diagnostic study in every patient.":["u171"],"copy|The progression in Figure 86-2 illustrates why visualizing a filling defect and removing it are separate decisions (p1344–1346).":["u172"],"copy|Table 86-6 concerns common-duct stones specifically.":["u173"],"copy|The book gives transabdominal ultrasound sensitivity of 20%–38% and specificity of 80%–100%, compared with 94% sensitivity and 95% specificity for endoscopic ultrasound.":["u174"],"copy|MRCP has reported sensitivity 57%–100% and specificity 93%–97%; it is diagnostic.":["u175"],"copy|ERCP can treat the obstruction but introduces procedural risks, including pancreatitis.":["u176"],"copy|Percutaneous transhepatic access can also be therapeutic and carries bleeding and pain risks (p1345).":["u177"],"copy|The earlier exam scenario of cholecystitis with acute pulmonary congestion and atrial fibrillation tests whether this patient is fit for immediate definitive surgery.":["u178"],"copy|Fluids and antimicrobials treat systemic consequences but may not control an infected obstructed gallbladder.":["u179"],"copy|Gallbladder drainage can provide source control when acute illness makes cholecystectomy unsafe.":["u180"],"copy|The intervention is chosen because of present physiological risk, not because a diagnosis of dementia by itself excludes surgery.":["u181"],"copy|For an appropriate operative candidate, early laparoscopic cholecystectomy can be preferable to waiting until complications develop.":["u182"],"copy|For asymptomatic uncomplicated stones, the balance is different (p1346).":["u183"],"copy|In Figure 86-3 the common bile duct and main pancreatic duct approach the duodenum at the pancreatic head.":["u184"],"copy|The position helps explain why an impacted stone can impair pancreatic drainage and why an obstructing lesion in the head can present with jaundice.":["u185"],"copy|The body and tail lie away from that outlet (p1347).":["u186"],"copy|Acinar cells supply enzymes while duct cells contribute bicarbonate-rich fluid.":["u187"],"copy|The latter buffers acidic contents delivered to the duodenum.":["u188"],"copy|Pancreatic secretory capacity has a reserve large enough that modest physiologic changes do not usually cause symptomatic maldigestion.":["u189"],"copy|The chapter's aging table describes increasing fat and fibrosis and a reduction in pancreatic weight after the seventh decade.":["u190"],"copy|Bicarbonate output falls after the fourth decade, and the duct in the head or body can become a little wider.":["u191"],"copy|For the earlier exam, both declining exocrine function and declining gland weight can be true; increasing fat does not imply that gland weight must increase.":["u192"],"copy|Steatorrhea or unexplained weight loss should still prompt investigation (p1347–1348).":["u193"],"copy|Acute epigastric pain radiating to the back, often accompanied by vomiting, suggests pancreatitis in an appropriate clinical setting.":["u194"],"copy|Lipase stays raised for longer than amylase, useful when presentation is delayed.":["u195"],"copy|Minor enzyme elevations also occur in other conditions, so interpret the magnitude and the clinical syndrome together.":["u196"],"copy|Ultrasound can identify a biliary cause.":["u197"],"copy|Additional cross-sectional imaging can clarify uncertain presentations, complications or an underlying lesion; the initial clinical diagnosis does not demand that every uncomplicated presentation be scanned solely to confirm a high enzyme value.":["u198"],"copy|Table 86-8 includes hypercalcemia, hypertriglyceridemia, drugs, trauma, duct obstruction and tumors alongside gallstones and alcohol (p1348–1349).":["u199"],"copy|Measure triglycerides early because fasting and IV fluids can lower the value rapidly.":["u200"],"copy|In a previously well older adult with no gallstones and no history of sustained heavy alcohol use, ask why the pancreatic duct became obstructed.":["u201"],"copy|A small tumor may cause 