nor attribution of loose stool to a common drug should close the assessment when weight is falling.":["u215"],"copy|Treat malabsorption with pancreatic enzyme supplementation; consider nutrition, glycemic management and cause of obstruction as well as pain.":["u216"],"copy|A tumor can mimic or cause chronic pancreatitis in an older person (p1349).":["u217"],"copy|Stage pancreatic adenocarcinoma for distant disease and vascular invasion before determining whether curative-intent resection is possible.":["u218"],"copy|CT and endoscopic ultrasound provide complementary information.":["u219"],"copy|A mass identified because of obstructive jaundice and an incidental tiny cyst require different workups.":["u220"],"copy|For a cyst, first ask about pancreatitis and communication with the pancreatic duct, then consider wall or nodule characteristics and whether further information would change the treatment of this particular patient.":["u221"],"copy|Fluid analysis assists classification; a mucinous result does not itself stage cancer (p1349–1350).":["u222"],"copy|Table 86-9 contrasts a post-pancreatitis pseudocyst with a serous lesion that can have a central scar and very low malignant potential.":["u223"],"copy|Mucinous cystic neoplasms may show peripheral calcification without duct communication; intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms communicate with the duct.":["u224"],"copy|The table gives cyst-fluid CEA above 192 ng/mL as a feature of the two mucinous groups and below 10 ng/mL for the serous group.":["u225"],"copy|These are fluid measurements used along with imaging and cytology, not a blood test for cancer.":["u226"],"copy|Main-duct involvement carries more concern than branch-duct disease in the book.":["u227"],"copy|Resection or surveillance depends on the type, degree of suspicion, surgical fitness and life expectancy (p1350).":["u228"],"copy|Figure 86-4 shows an endoscopic ultrasound-guided aspiration of a cyst ultimately identified as a serous cystadenoma.":["u229"],"copy|The image illustrates how fluid was obtained, not a rule that every incidental cyst requires puncture.":["u230"],"copy|Interpret fluid, duct anatomy, symptoms and imaging features together when deciding on surveillance, tissue assessment or referral (p1350).":["u231"],"copy|Earlier sittings, chapter-level mapping — 2022-Jun Q65, 2021-Dec Q39.":["u232"],"copy|1.":["u233"],"copy|Which liver test abnormalities should prompt investigation instead of attribution to age?":["u234"],"copy|2.":["u235"],"copy|Which component of drug metabolism is relatively preserved?":["u236"],"copy|3.":["u237"],"copy|Which antibody supports primary biliary cholangitis in cholestatic liver disease?":["u238"],"copy|4.":["u239"],"copy|How is suspected primary sclerosing cholangitis usually assessed?":["u240"],"copy|5.":["u241"],"copy|What should trigger a renewed search for bleeding, infection or medication effects in cirrhosis?":["u242"],"copy|6.":["u243"],"copy|Why might a patient stop taking lactulose?":["u244"],"copy|7.":["u245"],"copy|What distinguishes acute cholecystitis from ascending cholangitis anatomically?":["u246"],"copy|8.":["u247"],"copy|Which three findings suggest ascending cholangitis?":["u248"],"copy|9.":["u249"],"copy|What does a negative ultrasound mean for possible common-duct stones?":["u250"],"copy|10.":["u251"],"copy|How does ERCP differ from MRCP in the setting of obstruction?":["u252"],"copy|11.":["u253"],"copy|When is gallbladder drainage a reasonable alternative to immediate surgery?":["u254"],"copy|12.":["u255"],"copy|How do pancreatic weight, composition and bicarbonate secretion change with age?":["u256"],"copy|13.":["u257"],"copy|Why is lipase valuable when acute pancreatitis presents late?":["u258"],"copy|14.":["u259"],"copy|What diagnosis deserves particular attention in otherwise unexplained acute pancreatitis in an older person?":["u260"],"copy|15.":["u261"],"copy|What treatment targets malabsorption in chronic pancreatitis?":["u262"],"copy|Ans